Arsenal entered the competition in the third round and was drawn to play [[Watford F.C.|Watford]] of the [[Football League First Division|First Division]]. They took the lead in the eighth minute, where good play by [[Nwankwo Kanu]] allowed [[Thierry Henry]] to round goalkeeper [[Alec Chamberlain]] and tap the ball into the empty goal.<ref name="watford">{{cite news |title=Gilt-edged Arsenal give Golden Boys a lesson |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/jan/06/match.sport4 |newspaper=The Observer |location=London |first=Ian |last=Ridley |date=6 January 2002 |access-date=19 March 2013}}</ref> The lead was doubled two minutes later: Kanu again found Henry, who "unselfishly squared the ball to midfielder [[Freddie Ljungberg]] for another tap-in."<ref name="watford"/> [[Gifton Noel-Williams]] moments later halved the scoreline, heading the ball in from a [[Gary Fisken]] cross.<ref name="watford"/> After squandering numerous chances to increase their lead, Arsenal added a late third and fourth goal from Kanu and [[Dennis Bergkamp]], before [[Marcus Gayle]] scored what was a mere consolation for Watford in [[stoppage time]].<ref name="watford"/>

In the following round, Arsenal faced cup holders [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]], who had defeated them in the [[2001 FA Cup final|previous season's final]], at home. A solitary goal scored by Bergkamp in the 27th minute saw the home side progress and avenge their final defeat in a match layered with controversy: [[Martin Keown]], Bergkamp and Liverpool's [[Jamie Carragher]] were all [[Foul (association football)#Red card .28dismissal.29|sent off]] in the space of ten minutes, the latter for hurling back a coin at the crowd.<ref>{{cite news |title=Liverpool lost in red mist |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/2428868/Liverpool-lost-in-red-mist.html |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |first=Paul |last=Hayward |date=27 January 2002 |access-date=19 March 2013}}</ref> Against [[Gillingham F.C.|Gillingham]] in the fifth round, Arsenal twice had their lead cancelled out, before [[Tony Adams]] scored the winning goal of the match.<ref>{{cite news |title=Adams turns it around |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/feb/17/match.sport |newspaper=The Observer |location=London |first=Ian |last=Ridley |date=17 February 2002 |access-date=19 March 2013}}</ref>

Arsenal played [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]] in the sixth round on 9 March 2002. It was the second meeting between both teams in a week, and in spite of Arsenal winning the first fixture and scoring the opener in the cup tie, Newcastle held them to a 1–1 draw.<ref>{{cite news |title=Arsenal are let off the hook |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/newcastle-united/3019768/Arsenal-are-let-off-the-hook.html |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |first=Colin |last=Malam |date=9 March 2002 |access-date=19 March 2013}}</ref> A replay was scheduled two weeks later at noon. Arsenal won by three goals to nil, but during the match lost [[Robert Pires]] to injury; he was ruled out for the remainder of the season with medial knee ligament damage.<ref>{{cite news |title=Bergkamp calling the Toon |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/mar/24/match.sport |newspaper=The Observer |location=London |first=Amy |last=Lawrence |date=24 March 2002 |access-date=19 March 2013}}</ref> An own goal by [[Middlesbrough F.C.|Middlesbrough]]'s [[Gianluca Festa]], from an Henry free-kick, was enough for Arsenal to win the semi-final.<ref>{{cite news |title=Festa gift fires Gunners into final |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/apr/15/match.sport13 |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |first=David |last=Lacey |date=15 April 2002 |access-date=19 March 2013}}</ref>

