Chelsea's route to the final began in the third round, with a trip to [[Carrow Road]] to face [[Norwich City F.C.|Norwich City]]. An uneventful tie, with [[Carlo Cudicini]] making a series of saves to deny Norwich finished goalless and was replayed at [[Stamford Bridge (stadium)|Stamford Bridge]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Cudicini denies Norwich |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/1735044.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=5 January 2002 |access-date=28 September 2015}}</ref> Goals from [[Mario Stanić]] and [[Frank Lampard]] put Chelsea in a commanding lead and [[Gianfranco Zola]] scored the team's third with a unique piece of skill. From a corner, the Italian made a near-post run and flicked the ball airborne.<ref name="zoln">{{cite news |title=Zola adds lustre to Cup magic |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/fa-cup/4180222/Zola-adds-lustre-to-Cup-magic.html |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |first=Henry |last=Winter |date=16 January 2002 |access-date=6 October 2015}}</ref> Chelsea finished the match 4–0 winners and were drawn to face [[West Ham United F.C.|West Ham United]] in the next round.<ref name="zoln"/> [[Frédéric Kanouté]]'s late goal cancelled out [[Jimmy Floyd Hasselbaink]]'s opener for Chelsea and the tie was replayed at [[Boleyn Ground|Upton Park]] the following Wednesday night.<ref>{{cite news |title=Kanoute saves Hammers |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/1780989.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=26 January 2002 |access-date=28 September 2015}}</ref> West Ham went a goal up when [[Jermain Defoe]] scored for West Ham, but their lead was short-lived as Hasselbaink directly from a free-kick. Defoe restored the home side's advantage in the 50th minute, though substitute [[Mikael Forssell]] came on to equalise for Chelsea and in stoppage time [[John Terry]] headed-in goalwards from a corner to complete the visitors comeback.<ref>{{cite news |title=Terry strike stuns West Ham |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/2430128/Terry-strike-stuns-West-Ham.html |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |first=Henry |last=Winter |date=6 February 2002 |access-date=6 October 2015}}</ref>

In the fifth round Chelsea played [[Preston North End F.C.|Preston North End]] at home.<ref name="pne">{{cite news |title=Chelsea duo down Preston |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/1821080.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=17 February 2002 |access-date=28 September 2015}}</ref> The visitors started well and took the lead in the sixth minute through [[Richard Cresswell]]. Cudicini's timely save denied [[Jon Macken]] from extending Preston's lead soon after and Chelsea responded to the setback with an equaliser, scored by [[Eiður Guðjohnsen]].<ref name="pne"/> Chelsea led after 26 minutes, but came close to conceding late on when Macken was denied once again by Cudicini. Forssell then scored Chelsea's third to settle the home side's nerves.<ref name="pne"/>

Chelsea travelled to [[White Hart Lane]] to face [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] in the sixth round. The team finished the tie as comfortable 4–0 winners, never looking as though they would crumble once [[William Gallas]] scored inside 12 minutes.<ref name="spurs">{{cite news |title=Blues thrive on Petit's French polish |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/mar/11/match.sport9 |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |first=David |last=Lacey |date=11 March 2002 |access-date=28 September 2015}}</ref> The one negative from their performance was the dismissal of [[Graeme Le Saux]] for a second bookable offence in the second half.<ref name="spurs"/> [[West London derby|Local rivals]] [[Fulham F.C.|Fulham]] were Chelsea's semi-final opponents. A scrappy match, which saw Fulham dominate much of the play but creating little, was settled in Chelsea's favour. Terry scored just before half time, heading the ball through the legs of [[Louis Saha]] standing in Fulham's goal.<ref>{{cite news |title=Terry bundles the Blues to Cardiff |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/apr/15/match.sport12 |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |first=Jon |last=Brodkin |date=15 April 2002 |access-date=28 September 2015}}</ref>

==Pre-match==
Arsenal were appearing in the final of the FA Cup for the fifteenth time, and for the second consecutive year. They had won the cup seven times previously (in [[1930 FA Cup final|1930]], [[1936 FA Cup final|1936]], [[1950 FA Cup final|1950]], [[1971 FA Cup final|1971]], [[1979 FA Cup final|1979]], [[1993 FA Cup final|1993]] and [[1998 FA Cup final|1998]]) and were beaten in the final seven times, the most recent in [[2001 FA Cup final|last season's showpiece event]]. By comparison, Chelsea were making their seventh appearance in a FA Cup final. The club won the cup three times ([[1970 FA Cup final|1970]], [[1997 FA Cup final|1997]] and [[2000 UEFA Cup Final|2000]]) and lost the same number of finals ([[1915 FA Cup final|1915]], [[1967 FA Cup final|1967]] and [[1994 FA Cup final|1994]]). Arsenal and Chelsea had previously met fourteen times{{cn|date=October 2026}} in the FA Cup, including four replays. Arsenal had a slender advantage in those meetings, winning five times to Chelsea's four, and defeated their London rivals a season ago in the fifth round of the competition.<ref>{{cite news |first=John |last=Ley |title=FA Cup Final Statpack |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/3027137/FA-Cup-Final-Statpack.html |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=3 May 2002 |access-date=6 October 2015}}</ref>

The most recent meeting between the two teams was in the Premier League on [[Boxing Day]], when Arsenal came from behind to beat Chelsea.<ref>{{cite news |first=David |last=Lacey |title=Arsenal prevail by force of will |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/dec/27/match.sport |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=27 December 2001 |access-date=25 September 2015}}</ref> Arsenal were unbeaten domestically since December 2001 and on course to complete their first league and cup double in four years.<ref name="wen">{{cite news |first=John |last=Ley |title=FA Cup Final: Arsenal ponder Wright decision |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/arsenal/3026966/FA-Cup-Final-Arsenal-ponder-Wright-decision.html |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=1 May 2002 |access-date=25 September 2015}}</ref> Arsenal manager [[Arsène Wenger]] was buoyant his team would complete the task on hand: "We have been facing cup games in the league every week for a long time and this is just another. Chelsea will be a difficult team to beat if they are at their best on Saturday, but such is the confidence in this squad, we just feel we can win every game."<ref name="nort">{{cite news |first=Gideon |last=Brooks |title=Wenger has final say in war of words |page=40 |newspaper=The Northern Echo |location=Middlesbrough |date=1 May 2002}}</ref> When asked what he made of [[Alex Ferguson|Sir Alex Ferguson]]'s comments that [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] played the best football in England, Wenger retorted: "What do you want me to say? Everybody thinks he has the prettiest wife."<ref name="nort"/> The Arsenal manager was undecided whether to drop [[Richard Wright (footballer)|Richard Wright]] who started in every round of the FA Cup for [[David Seaman]] and to recall [[Sol Campbell]] who recovered from a hamstring injury.<ref name="wen"/>
[[File:Millennium Stadium North.jpg|thumb|upright|The final was staged at the [[Millennium Stadium]] for the second consecutive year.]]
Chelsea manager [[Claudio Ranieri]] felt his team's participation in the cup final showed "...&nbsp;we are building something. It gives the young players confidence."<ref name="ran">{{cite news |first=Charlie |last=Norton |title=FA Cup Final: A process of progress for Ranieri |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/chelsea/3026967/FA-Cup-Final-A-process-of-progress-for-Ranieri.html |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=1 May 2002 |access-date=25 September 2015}}</ref> He noted their defence conceded fewer goals than the previous season, and targeted an improvement to their away record for the next campaign. Ranieri described the match against Arsenal as evenly balanced, adding: "Winning the FA Cup would make up for missing out on the [[UEFA Champions League|Champions League]]. If the team can win, they will believe in themselves, but if they don't, it won't be the worst setback to the building process."<ref name="ran"/> Le Saux resumed training having been absent with a calf injury, but Hasselbaink was a doubt for the final with a similar problem.<ref>{{cite news |first=Bill |last=Edgar |title=Ranieri keen to escape Arsenal's shadow |page=45 |newspaper=The Times |location=London |date=5 May 2002}}</ref>

The semi-finals at [[Villa Park]] and [[Old Trafford]] presented traffic problems and lengthy delays for supporters going to and from the grounds. The Mayor of London, [[Ken Livingstone]] had written to the FA, expressing his concern about the possible travel chaos and urging to be kept briefed on the arrangements.<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=Casey |title=Livingstone fears travel chaos at Cardiff final |page=26 |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=17 April 2002}}</ref> Train tickets were sold out since the semi-final round, despite the addition of services to accommodate 7,000 extra passengers.<ref name="dyft">{{cite news |first=Ben |last=Webster |title=Fans off to the Cup Final on a wing and a prayer |page=1 |newspaper=The Times |location=London |date=4 May 2002}}</ref> Both clubs laid on six planes to take its supporters directly to Cardiff at £135 each.<ref name="dyft"/> Although the [[M4 motorway|M4]] was busy on the day of the final, there was little traffic with no major delays. A spokesman for South Wales Police reported: "The motorways are clear, despite the predictions. People seem to have taken our advice and left early."<ref>{{cite news |title=Cup final traffic chaos averted |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/uk/1966385.stm |work=BBC News |date=4 May 2002 |access-date=25 September 2015}}</ref>

As with last season's event, the final was scheduled before the end of the domestic season. This was partly because the Premier League chose to end its campaign a week later, but with the [[2002 World Cup|World Cup]] starting on 31 May, the FA wanted to give players considerable time to prepare for the finals.<ref>{{cite news |first=Graeme |last=Sharp |url=http://www.thefreelibrary.com/In+the+Blue+corner%3A+CUPFINAL+SHOULDBE+SEASON'S+CLIMAX.-a085489767 |title=In the blue corner |newspaper=Liverpool Echo |date=4 May 2002 |access-date=27 September 2015}}</ref> This season's staging of the competition offered a greater financial incentive to clubs, given the [[BBC]] and [[Sky Sports]] signed a joint-deal with the FA, worth £400 million to broadcast matches.<ref name="conn">{{cite news |first=David |last=Conn |title=Auntie puts on her frills for the passion show |page=22|newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=4 January 2002}}</ref> Finalists stood to receive £1 million in prize money; the winners would pocket an extra million with additional TV revenues.<ref name="conn"/> The BBC spent a million promoting the FA Cup,<ref>{{cite news |first1=Gary |last1=Jacob |first2=Adam |last2=Sherwin |title=Cup fever fades as BBC viewers abandon final |page=2 |newspaper=The Times |location=London |date=7 May 2002}}</ref> and as part of their pre-match coverage included a sketch featuring [[Ricky Gervais]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Alyson |last=Rudd |title=Auntie puts on her frills for the passion show |page=32 |newspaper=The Times |location=London |date=6 May 2002}}</ref> Seat prices for the final exceeded £70, with some ticket touts charging as much as £600 outside the stadium.<ref name="wenn">{{cite news |title=Weng's men united in joy |page=46 |newspaper=The Northern Echo |location=Middlesbrough |date=6 May 2002}}</ref>

For their pre-match walkabout, the Arsenal players wore [[Hugo Boss]] suits, whereas the Chelsea players were suited in [[Armani]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/may/04/facup.sport |title=Why it's Brand Arsenal versus Lifestyle Chelsea |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=4 May 2002 |access-date=27 September 2015}}</ref> Chelsea were allocated the south dressing room after a coin-toss; it was considered a "jinx" given the last nine football teams to use it had failed to win.<ref>{{cite news |first=Robert |last=Mendick |title=Stadium hoodoo strikes again |page=1 |newspaper=The Independent on Sunday |location=London |date=5 May 2002}}</ref> The teams emerged from the tunnel once the traditional pre-match anthem "[[Abide with Me]]" was performed and Chelsea were led by midfielder [[Roberto Di Matteo]], who retired earlier in the season through injury.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.standard.co.uk/sport/football/claudio-ranieri-urges-roberto-di-matteo-not-to-tinker-with-chelsea-old-boys-7657317.html |title=Claudio Ranieri urges Roberto di Matteo not to tinker with Chelsea old boys |newspaper=London Evening Standard |date=18 April 2012 |access-date=27 September 2015}}</ref> As the national anthem was sung by sextet Tenors and Divas, the Arsenal players and Wenger shuffled together and linked arms in a show of unity.<ref name="wenn"/><ref>{{cite news |title=Final chance of fame for opera unknowns |page=42 |newspaper=The Northern Echo |location=Middlesbrough |date=4 May 2002}}</ref>

==Match==

===Team selection===
On the day of the final Terry woke up with a virus which affected his balance.<ref>{{cite news |first=Neil |last=Silver |title=Wonder goals put the Gunners on course for their third double |page=4 |newspaper=Edinburgh Evening News |date=4 May 2002}}</ref> Although he passed a fitness test in the morning, Ranieri decided to start him on the bench, to which the defender later reflected: "It was a tough decision but he did what he felt was right. It seems like somebody up there doesn't like me."<ref>{{cite book |first=Oliver |last=Derbyshire |title=JT – Captain, Leader, Legend: The Biography of John Terry |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=QYIxBwAAQBAJ&pg=PT79 |page=79 |publisher=John Blake Publishing |year=2010 |isbn=978-1-78418-484-1}}</ref> The teamsheets showed Gallas partnering [[Marcel Desailly]] in central defence, and Hasselbaink starting up front for Chelsea.<ref name="suntj">{{cite news|first=Joe |last=Lovejoy |title=Parlour strike sparks Chelsea downfall |newspaper=The Sunday Times |location=London |page=S3 |date=5 May 2002}}</ref> Wright was named on the bench for Arsenal and [[Ray Parlour]] was positioned alongside Vieira in central midfield.<ref name="suntj"/> Both teams lined up in a [[4–4–2 formation]]: a four-man [[Defender (association football)|defence]] (comprising two [[centre-back]]s and [[Full-back (association football)|left and right full-backs]]), four [[midfielders]] (two in the [[central midfielder|centre]], and one on each [[Wide midfielder|wing]]) and two [[centre-forward]]s.<ref>{{cite news |first=Catherine |last=Riley |title=Man-to-man marking |newspaper=The Times |location=London |page=21 |date=6 May 2002}}</ref>

===Summary===
Within a minute of Chelsea kicking off the match, Le Saux was booked for a challenge on [[Lauren (Cameroonian footballer)|Lauren]].<ref name="clockwatch">{{cite news |title=FA Cup final clockwatch |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/1965793.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=4 May 2002 |access-date=6 October 2015}}</ref> Arsenal were awarded the first corner of the game in the eight minutes later, but nothing came out of it as Adams fouled [[Mario Melchiot]] in the penalty box.<ref name="clockwatch"/> The opening half-hour was mostly event-free, with neither side dominating and few goalscoring opportunities fashioned. Chelsea adopted a tactic of narrowing the pitch and using little width, which sedated Arsenal's typically fluent football. Guðjohnsen tested the Arsenal defence by making dangerous runs, but one in the 12th minute was ruled as a foul.<ref name="clockwatch"/> Arsenal's first chance came a minute later when Henry used his pace to run towards the Chelsea goal. He set up [[Sylvain Wiltord]], whose shot was blocked by Desailly.<ref name="clockwatch"/> Vieira struggled to match the energetic performance of his opponent [[Frank Lampard]] and in the 17th minute Vieira's careless pass was intercepted by the England midfielder. Lampard decided to take a shot, forcing a save from Seaman.<ref name="clockwatch"/> Four minutes later Vieira started a move which almost gave Arsenal the lead. A ball over the top found Bergkamp in the Chelsea area, but he guided his header just wide.<ref name="clockwatch"/> In the 26th minute Vieira was awarded the final's first yellow card for a foul on Guðjohnsen.<ref name="clockwatch"/> A confrontation between Melchiot and [[Freddie Ljungberg]] occurred in the 33rd minute, but referee [[Mike Riley (referee)|Mike Riley]] decided not to brandish a card, instead choosing to have a few words with the players.<ref name="clockwatch"/> Campbell's failed clearance a minute later presented Guðjohnsen with goal-scoring opportunity, but he hit his shot directly at Seaman. As the match drew nearer to half-time, Arsenal started to find their rhythm and played their usual passing game.<ref name="sunind">{{cite news|first=Nick |last=Townsend |title=Terry's misery is complete as Ljungberg dances into the record books and launches the Double party |newspaper=The Independent on Sunday |location=London |page=S3 |date=5 May 2002}}</ref> They created the best chance of the first half, when a cross from Wiltord found Lauren, who headed the ball just over the crossbar.<ref name="suntj"/> Hasselbaink, largely ineffective as he was blighted with injury, combined with Guðjohnsen to split open the Arsenal defence, but the move was halted as Riley called offside.<ref name="clockwatch"/>

[[Celestine Babayaro]], who had been struggling with an injury, played no further part in the final and was substituted before the second half commenced.<ref name="suntim">{{cite news| first=Rob |last=Hughes |title=Wenger revels in Double whammy |newspaper=The Sunday Times |location=London |page=S2 |date=5 May 2002}}</ref> Terry came on in his place to partner Desailly, which meant Gallas moved to left-back.<ref name="suntim"/> Arsenal resumed play and a shot by Henry was kept out by Cudicini.<ref name="sunind"/> The scare brought Chelsea to life and resulted in the team enjoying their best spell of the match. Guðjohnsen's effort in the 57th minute forced a save from Seaman, who tipped the ball over the bar.<ref name="sunind"/> [[Jesper Grønkjær]] then roamed forward and played the ball to Le Saux, but the defender's shot went well over.<ref name="clockwatch"/> Chelsea continued to pile pressure on Arsenal; Grønkjær's pass intended for Hasselbaink in the 61st minute was intercepted just in time by Adams and Melchiot's header unsettled Seaman in goal.<ref name="clockwatch"/> Wiltord then collected the ball from midfield and played a one-two with Henry, but directed his shot wide from the left flank.<ref name="obs">{{cite news|first=Paul |last=Wilson |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2002/may/05/match.sport |title=Ray of light before Freddie's final flourish |newspaper=The Observer |location=London |date=5 May 2002 |access-date=9 October 2015}}</ref> Chelsea made their second change in the 67th minute, bringing on Zola for Hasselbaink.<ref name="obs"/> The substitution did not have the desired effect as Arsenal went a goal ahead. Adams cleared the Chelsea danger and Wiltord's reverse pass found Parlour with acres of space to manoeuvre. The midfielder advanced as the Chelsea defence backed off and looked up before curling the ball from 25 yards.<ref name="obs"/> His effort went over a diving Cudicini, into the top right-hand corner of the Chelsea goal.<ref name="sunind"/>

Wenger made a defensive-minded change almost immediately, taking Bergkamp off for Edu.<ref name="clockwatch"/> A clash between Henry and Terry in the 75th minute resulted in both players receiving a yellow card for unsporting behaviour.<ref name="sunind"/> Winger [[Boudewijn Zenden]] replaced Melchiot a minute later; the attacking change altered Chelsea's positioning. With 10 minutes of normal time remaining Arsenal extended their lead, when Ljungberg scored. A similarly executed goal to Parlour's, the Swede ran forward, evaded the challenge of Terry before curling the ball past Cudicini from the edge of the penalty area.<ref name="suntj"/> Ljungberg was serenaded by the Arsenal crowd, who chanted "We love you Freddie, 'cos you've got red hair."<ref name="her">{{cite news|first=Karen |last=Giles |url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P2-23500145.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160409205239/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P2-23500145.html |url-status=usurped |archive-date=9 April 2016 |title=Arsenal keep clear heads for Old Trafford showdown |newspaper=The Herald |location=Glasgow |date=5 May 2002 |access-date=9 October 2015|via=[[HighBeam Research]] |url-access=subscription }}</ref> Chelsea struggled to find a response; Guðjohnsen's foul on Parlour late on highlighted the team's frustrations.<ref name="henr">{{cite news |title=Arsenal punish crass errors of Chelsea coach |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/chelsea/3027202/Arsenal-punish-crass-errors-of-Chelsea-coach.html |first=Henry |last=Winter |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=5 May 2002 |access-date=25 September 2015}}</ref> Riley blew for full-time after normal and stoppage time and the on-pitch interviews commenced. Once Arsenal received their medals, Adams was given the cup and he shared the honour of lifting it with Vieira, his stand-in captain.<ref name="glenm">{{cite news| first=Glenn |last=Moore |title=Wenger finds formula to turn Arsenal into winners |newspaper=The Independent |page=3 |date=6 May 2002 }}</ref>

